    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65574",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$",
      "worked_solution": "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
      "answer_fields": [
