    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-327988",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine",
      "problem": "Express $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})≈1.061\\text{ }\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)≈\\sqrt{21}\\,sin\\,(6x-1.061).\n$$",
